#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198784 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198269 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "efdb77bf-dd8b-5ccc-8265-79bd319afaa3" :knowledge-point/title "Sets Defined Using Inequalities"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "535d3309-5331-5c9e-b19c-1fa3c96ffd57" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A$ and $C$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285556} {:answer/id #uuid "75a6e5c3-a45f-5d03-aabc-eb0b6a1107e7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$B$ and $C$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285881} {:answer/id #uuid "965bfa80-988a-59db-9277-376319bce8e0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286153} {:answer/id #uuid "9e6417ed-e697-5bc4-8225-43c66027e341" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$C$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286238} {:answer/id #uuid "ffe6c2e9-6e70-5866-8d35-2e64bcfb3540" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A$ and $B$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287135}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "535d3309-5331-5c9e-b19c-1fa3c96ffd57" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A$ and $C$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285556} :answer-field/id #uuid "fd3a634c-09d5-5706-a3dc-d30b317cf943" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287660}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e9482778-6f45-5e93-913d-289acb526444" :question/math-academy-id "q-181536" :question/problem "Which of the following sets are infinite?\n\nI. $A={n\\in Z\\,:\\,{n}^{2}-5n+6>0}$\n\nII. $B={m\\in Z\\,:\\,{m}^{2}-5m+6<0}$\n\nIII. $C={q\\in Q\\,:\\,{q}^{2}-5q+6<0}$" :question/worked-solution "The cardinality of a finite set is the number of elements contained within it. A set is infinite if it has infinitely many elements. \n\nLet's start by analyzing the parabola $y={x}^{2}-5x+6$ for $x\\in R.$\n\nFirst, we find the roots of the parabola:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-5x+6 & =0 \\\\ (x-2)(x-3) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the roots are $x=2$ and $x=3,$ and we can graph the parabola as follows:\n\n![](images/0a/0a42815f626652c332862a38a3812463a9407605329bd81df25f11274b26ce6f.png)\n\nTherefore:\n\n- The solution to the inequality ${x}^{2}-5x+6>0$ for $x\\in R$ is \n\n$$\nx<2\\,\\text{or}\\,x>3\n$$\n\nand can be expressed as $x\\in {I}_{1}=(-\\infty ,2)\\cup (3,\\infty ).$\n\n- Similarly, the solution to the inequality ${x}^{2}-5x+6<0$ for $x\\in R$ is \n\n$$\n2<x<3\n$$\n\nand can be expressed as $x\\in {I}_{2}=(2,3).$\n\nWith these in mind, let's examine our sets:\n\n- The set $A$ is infinite. If $n\\in Z,$ the solutions to the inequality ${n}^{2}-5n+6>0$ are all the integers that lie in the interval ${I}_{1}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\nA={…,0,1}\\cup {4,5,…}.\n$$\n\n- The set $B$ is finite. If $m\\in Z,$ the solutions to the inequality ${m}^{2}-5m+6<0$ are all the integers that lie in the interval ${I}_{2}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\nB={}.\n$$\n\nConsequently, $|B|=0,$ which is finite.\n\n- The set $C$ is infinite. If $q\\in Q,$ the solutions to ${q}^{2}-5q+6<0$ are all the rational numbers that lie in the interval ${I}_{2}.$ Since any real interval of nonzero length contains infinitely many rational numbers, it follows that $C$ is infinite.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$A$ and $C$ only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198786 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198270 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f98332fd-e902-5390-a4d3-4e181ce97219" :knowledge-point/title "Sets Defined Using Trigonometric Equations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "20aae210-e0f7-5b7c-8fd7-140a4335ba79" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A$ and $B$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285082} {:answer/id #uuid "3373e54a-be35-5efd-90b6-ec8c2cca7b0c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A$ and $C$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285258} {:answer/id #uuid "5e01deeb-14eb-544d-be9e-b79f8a53639d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285651} {:answer/id #uuid "77f050d8-fe63-5e9f-9fbf-27d8e846cd9c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$B$ and $C$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285905} {:answer/id #uuid "e640f85e-540e-5f14-922f-9f4e38fbef6e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$C$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286895}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "77f050d8-fe63-5e9f-9fbf-27d8e846cd9c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$B$ and $C$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285905} :answer-field/id #uuid "e65de60f-3093-5869-90cf-a3f74397b434" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287622}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "4e83b937-f1ab-5064-ab2b-f81fab22743d" :question/math-academy-id "q-181597" :question/problem "Which of the following sets are infinite?\n\n- $A={n\\in Z\\,:\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2n\\pi )=1}$\n- $B={q\\in Q\\,:\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2q\\pi )=1}$\n- $C={x\\in R\\,:\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x\\pi )=1}$" :question/worked-solution "The cardinality of a finite set is the number of elements contained within it. A set is infinite if it has infinitely many elements. \n\nWe start by finding the solutions to the equation $\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x\\pi )=1$ for $x\\in R:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x\\pi  & =\\frac{\\pi }{2}+2m\\pi  \\\\ x & =\\frac{1}{4}+m,\\,m\\in Z\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our sets:\n\n- The set $A$ is finite. There are no values of $m\\in Z$ such that $\\frac{1}{4}+m\\in Z.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\nA={}.\n$$\n\nConsequently, $|A|=0,$ which is finite.\n\n- The set $B$ is infinite. Listing the elements of $B$ for different values of $m,$ we have\n\n$$\nB={…,-\\frac{3}{4},\\frac{1}{4},\\frac{5}{4},\\frac{9}{4},…}.\n$$\n\n- The set $C$ is infinite. Since $B$ is infinite, and $B⊆C,$ it follows that $C$ is infinite.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$B$ and $C$ only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198787 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198271 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "26758016-ed47-562a-9338-85c71ffbeaa9" :knowledge-point/title "Sets Defined Using Derivatives"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1084b01d-7545-5c95-b9a0-56ef0389ff00" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A$ and $B$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284931} {:answer/id #uuid "18c26ab8-61bf-5c33-a9f4-1a50b4004ea9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$B$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285007} {:answer/id #uuid "32df84de-5e40-5d3d-aabe-e234be9de339" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$C$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285252} {:answer/id #uuid "54c8ef2f-4beb-5c37-8311-c401c1bc180b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A$ and $C$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285565} {:answer/id #uuid "e307f8e5-16ab-586d-8923-d236690f9083" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$B$ and $C$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286860}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e307f8e5-16ab-586d-8923-d236690f9083" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$B$ and $C$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286860} :answer-field/id #uuid "e687931f-2924-5562-80cc-127a7522cc68" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287623}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e02e6bd4-a148-57c6-a612-60228a140b9e" :question/math-academy-id "q-181599" :question/problem "Which of the following sets are infinite?\n\n- $A={{f}^{(n)}(x)=\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{n}}{\\text{d}{x}^{n}}({x}^{2})\\,:\\,n\\in {N}_{0}}$\n- $B={{g}^{(n)}(x)=\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{n}}{\\text{d}{x}^{n}}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)\\,:\\,n\\in {N}_{0}}$\n- $C={{h}^{(n)}(x)=\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{n}}{\\text{d}{x}^{n}}(\\sqrt{x})\\,:\\,n\\in {N}_{0}}$" :question/worked-solution "The cardinality of a finite set is the number of elements contained within it. A set is infinite if it has infinitely many elements. \n\n- The set $A$ is finite. Listing a few elements of our set, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{(0)}(x) & ={x}^{2} \\\\ {f}^{(1)}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{2})=2x \\\\ {f}^{(2)}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2x)=2 \\\\ {f}^{(3)}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2)=0 \\\\ {f}^{(4)}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(0)=0 \\\\  & ⋮\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, for $n>3,$ we have ${f}^{(n)}(x)=0.$ Listing a few elements of our set, we have the following:\n\n$$\nA={{x}^{2},2x,2,0}.\n$$\n\nConsequently, $|A|=4,$ which is finite.\n\n- The set $B$ is finite. Listing the first few elements of our set, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}^{(0)}(x) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡x=\\operatorname{ln}⁡x \\\\ {g}^{(1)}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)=\\frac{1}{x} \\\\ {g}^{(2)}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{x})=-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}} \\\\ {g}^{(3)}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}})=\\frac{2}{{x}^{3}} \\\\ ⋮\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we can write our set as follows:\n\n$$\nB={\\operatorname{ln}⁡x,\\frac{1}{x},-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}},-\\frac{2}{{x}^{3}},…}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $B$ is infinite.\n\n- The set $C$ is infinite. Similar to the previous case, listing the first few elements of our set, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{h}^{(0)}(x) & =\\sqrt{x} \\\\ {h}^{(1)}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\sqrt{x})=\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{x}} \\\\ {h}^{(2)}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{x}})=-\\frac{1}{4\\sqrt{{x}^{3}}} \\\\ {h}^{(3)}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(-\\frac{1}{4\\sqrt{{x}^{3}}})=\\frac{3}{8\\sqrt{{x}^{5}}} \\\\ ⋮\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we can write our set as follows:\n\n$$\nC={\\sqrt{x},\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{x}},-\\frac{1}{4\\sqrt{{x}^{3}}},\\frac{3}{8\\sqrt{{x}^{5}}},…}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $C$ is infinite.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$B$ and $C$ only.\""}]}
